---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Framing Hammers",
    "url": "/best-framing-hammer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Circular Saws",
    "url": "/best-cordless-circular-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Miter Saws",
    "url": "/best-miter-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Impact Drivers",
    "url": "/best-impact-driver/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Tool Brand",
    "url": "/best-cordless-tool-brand/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Band Saws",
    "url": "/best-band-saw/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "7 Best Framing Nailers: Cordless, Pneumatic and Fuel-Cell Picks";
const description =
  "The best framing nailers compared on nail range, collation angle, weight and power source, from cordless Milwaukee and DeWalt to pneumatic Metabo HPT and Paslode.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2744-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 21 Degree Framing Nailer (2744-20)",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Cordless, 21° Plastic Collated, 2 to 3-1/2 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The M18 FUEL 2744-20 is the cordless framing nailer we would buy first if you already own Milwaukee batteries, because it covers the full framing nail range without a hose, a compressor or a fuel cell. Retailers list it for 21° plastic-collated full round-head nails from 2 to 3-1/2 inches with a shank range of .113 to .148 inch, which means it will drive the 16d sinkers and 3-1/4 inch nails most framing codes call for and still reach the 3-1/2 inch nails some engineers specify. Listings give a 51-nail magazine, a firing rate of 3 nails per second, and both sequential and contact (bump) actuation, so you can place nails carefully on layout or bump-fire sheathing. The catch is weight: retailers list the bare tool at 9.4 lb in most listings and as much as 10.05 lb in one, before you add a battery, so it is noticeably heavier than a pneumatic nailer at the end of a long day. Listing prices for the bare tool run roughly $399 to $449.",
    "pros": [
      "Retailers list a 2 to 3-1/2 in nail range at .113 to .148 in shank",
      "Sequential and bump-fire modes, listed at 3 nails per second",
      "51-nail magazine per retailer listings",
      "No hose, compressor or fuel cell to manage",
      "Runs on M18 batteries shared with a huge tool line"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Bare tool listed at 9.4 to 10.05 lb before the battery",
      "Bare-tool price of about $399 to $449 is high if you are not on M18",
      "Heavier than the pneumatic picks for overhead work"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you frame without a compressor and own M18 batteries, the 2744-20 is the cordless nailer that covers the full 2 to 3-1/2 inch range with bump fire, at the cost of extra weight."
  },
  {
    "id": "metabo-hpt-nr90aes1",
    "name": "Metabo HPT NR90AES1 Pneumatic Framing Nailer",
    "badge": "Best Pneumatic",
    "category": "Pneumatic, 21° Plastic Collated, 2 to 3-1/2 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The NR90AES1 is the pneumatic framing nailer we point most people toward, because it is light, reliable in design, and inexpensive compared with any cordless option. Retailers describe it as a 21° plastic-collated full round-head nailer that drives nails from 2 to 3-1/2 inches with a .113 to .148 inch shank range, and list a 64-nail magazine and a 70 to 120 PSI operating range on a 3/8 inch NPT inlet. One retailer lists the tool at 7.5 lb, and Ace Hardware lists the newer NR90AES1M version at 7 lb, so it is two to three pounds lighter than a cordless nailer without its battery. That weight difference is the whole case for pneumatic framing: it matters on walls, it matters more on rafters, and it is why many crews still drag a hose. The obvious trade-off is that you need a compressor that can keep up and a hose to manage. Check the suffix when you buy, because the NR90AES1 and NR90AES1M are sold under similar listings.",
    "pros": [
      "Retail listings give 7 to 7.5 lb, far lighter than cordless nailers",
      "2 to 3-1/2 in plastic-collated nails at .113 to .148 in",
      "64-nail magazine per retailer listings",
      "Much cheaper than a cordless framing nailer",
      "Simple pneumatic design with few parts to fail"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a compressor and hose",
      "Hose management slows roof and ladder work",
      "NR90AES1 and NR90AES1M are sold under similar names"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For anyone who already owns a compressor, the NR90AES1 is the lightest, cheapest way to drive full-length 21° framing nails all day."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcn21pl",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX XR 21° Cordless Framing Nailer (DCN21PL)",
    "badge": "Best for DeWalt Owners",
    "category": "Cordless, 21° Plastic Collated, 2 to 3-1/4 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The DCN21PL is DeWalt's 21° plastic-collated version of its brushless cordless framing nailer, and the obvious choice if your batteries are yellow. Retail listings give a fastener range of 2 to 3-1/4 inches and a nail diameter of .113 to .148 inch, a 49-nail magazine, and a tool weight of 8.5 lb with the battery installed. That 3-1/4 inch ceiling is the main limit next to the Milwaukee: it covers 16d sinkers and the common 3-1/4 inch framing nail, but not 3-1/2 inch nails. The bare tool (DCN21PLB) is listed at $369 by one retailer, and kit prices with a 4.0Ah battery and charger run from about $429 to $499 depending on the seller. Pro Tool Reviews noted at launch that the 21° version holds fewer nails than DeWalt's 30° model, from 55 down to 49, and weighs slightly more, which is worth knowing if you are choosing between DeWalt's two collation angles.",
    "pros": [
      "Listed at 8.5 lb with battery, lighter than the Milwaukee bare",
      "2 to 3-1/4 in nails at .113 to .148 in",
      "Runs on DeWalt 20V MAX batteries",
      "Kit with 4.0Ah battery and charger widely sold"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tops out at 3-1/4 in nails, not 3-1/2 in",
      "49-nail magazine is smaller than DeWalt's 30° model",
      "Kit pricing varies widely between retailers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For DeWalt 20V owners framing with 21° nails up to 3-1/4 inches, the DCN21PL is the natural pick and lighter in hand than the Milwaukee."
  },
  {
    "id": "paslode-cfn325xp",
    "name": "Paslode Cordless XP 30° Framing Nailer (CFN325XP)",
    "badge": "Lightest Cordless",
    "category": "Gas/Battery, 30° Paper Collated, 2 to 3-1/4 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Paslode's Cordless XP is the original fuel-cell framing nailer, and Paslode calls it the lightest cordless framing nailer on the market at 7.2 lb with its battery. Instead of a motor or an air spring, it fires a small charge of fuel from a disposable cell, and Paslode rates it at about 1,200 nails per fuel cell and about 9,000 nails per battery charge. Coastal Tool lists a nail range of 2 to 3-1/4 inches, a .113 to .131 inch shank range, and 30° paper-collated clipped-head nails, with a firing rate of 2 to 3 nails per second in sequential mode. That combination makes it a favorite for punch-out work, blocking and remodels where you move constantly and want a light tool. The downsides are ongoing fuel-cell cost, the smell of exhaust indoors, and a magazine that holds only one strip of nails at a time, which Pro Tool Reviews calls out as its main weakness. Coastal Tool says it works down to 14°F with Paslode's All-Season fuel.",
    "pros": [
      "Paslode lists 7.2 lb with battery, the lightest cordless option here",
      "Rated for about 1,200 nails per fuel cell and 9,000 per charge",
      "Long track record on job sites",
      "All-Season fuel rated down to 14°F per retailer listing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fuel cells are an ongoing cost",
      "Holds only one strip of nails at a time",
      "30° paper-collated clipped-head nails only",
      "Exhaust smell indoors"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If weight matters most and you do short bursts of framing on the move, the Paslode is the lightest cordless framing nailer listed, as long as you accept buying fuel cells."
  },
  {
    "id": "metabo-hpt-nr3690dr",
    "name": "Metabo HPT 36V MultiVolt Framing Nailer (NR3690DR)",
    "badge": "Best Air-Spring Cordless",
    "category": "Cordless 36V, 21° Plastic Collated, 2 to 3-1/2 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The NR3690DR is Metabo HPT's 36V MultiVolt framing nailer, and its selling point is consistency: it uses an air-spring drive rather than a flywheel, so there is very little ramp-up delay between trigger pull and drive. Metabo HPT's own product page lists it as a 21° round-head tool for plastic strip collated nails from 2 to 3-1/2 inches with a 0.113 to 0.131 inch diameter, and rates it at about 900 nails per charge on the included 2.5Ah MultiVolt battery or 1,200 on a 4.0Ah pack, with a driving speed of 3 nails per second. Pro Tool Reviews weighed the bare tool at 9.3 lb and 10.8 lb with the 2.5Ah battery, and JLC Online reported almost 11 lb with a battery installed. Note that the 30° clipped-head version is a different model, the NR3690DC, so match the model number to your nails. It is a strong buy for Metabo HPT MultiVolt users; for anyone else the battery buy-in is significant.",
    "pros": [
      "Metabo HPT lists 2 to 3-1/2 in nails",
      "Rated around 900 nails per 2.5Ah charge, 1,200 per 4.0Ah",
      "Air-spring drive with little firing delay",
      "3 nails per second per Metabo HPT"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Reviewers report 10.8 to nearly 11 lb with battery",
      "Shank range listed only to 0.131 in",
      "21° and 30° versions use different model numbers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For MultiVolt owners who want a cordless nailer that fires instantly across the full nail range, the NR3690DR is the one, if you can live with the weight."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-pbl345",
    "name": "Ryobi 18V ONE+ HP Brushless AirStrike 21° Framing Nailer (PBL345)",
    "badge": "Best Value Cordless",
    "category": "Cordless 18V, 21° Plastic Collated, 2 to 3-1/2 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Ryobi's PBL345 is the cheapest route to a cordless framing nailer from a big battery platform, and it is aimed at homeowners building decks, sheds and the occasional wall rather than daily framing crews. Ryobi's Canadian site lists a 2 to 3-1/2 inch nail range for 21° full round-head nails, and Pro Tool Reviews gives a nail gauge of 0.113 to 0.131 inch. Ryobi rates it for up to 750 nails per charge on a 4Ah High Performance battery, and lists a 33-nail magazine, which is small and means frequent reloads. Pro Tool Reviews weighed it at 9.3 lb bare and 10.9 lb with a 4.0Ah battery. It uses an onboard air tank that you can top up through a standard air valve, with a recommended maximum of 125 PSI, and it carries Ryobi's 3-year warranty. Ryobi's own site lists it at $299 while other retailers show higher kit prices.",
    "pros": [
      "Ryobi lists $299 for the tool, the lowest cordless price here",
      "2 to 3-1/2 in nail range per Ryobi",
      "Up to 750 nails per 4Ah charge, per Ryobi",
      "Runs on widely sold Ryobi ONE+ batteries",
      "3-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "33-nail magazine means frequent reloading",
      "10.9 lb with battery per Pro Tool Reviews",
      "Built for occasional use rather than daily framing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a homeowner on Ryobi batteries building a deck or a shed, the PBL345 is the most affordable cordless framing nailer that still drives full-length nails."
  },
  {
    "id": "bostitch-f21pl",
    "name": "Bostitch F21PL Round Head Framing Nailer",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pneumatic",
    "category": "Pneumatic, 21° Plastic Collated, 2 to 3-1/2 in Nails",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The F21PL is a long-running pneumatic 21° framing nailer and a common budget pick for anyone with a compressor. One distributor lists framing nails from 2 to 3-1/2 inches and metal connector nails from 1-1/2 to 2-1/2 inches, because the tool ships with two quick-change nosepieces, one for framing and one for metal connectors such as joist hangers. Specs list 70 to 120 PSI operating pressure. Weight is less clear: a retailer spec sheet gives 3.7 kg, about 8.2 lb, while Pro Tool Reviews weighed the related F21PL2 at 8.8 lb and called it a little heavy, so expect it to be heavier than the Metabo HPT pneumatic. The appeal is the price and the metal-connector nosepiece, which saves buying a separate positive-placement nailer if you only hang a few joist hangers.",
    "pros": [
      "Framing and metal-connector nosepieces included per distributor listings",
      "2 to 3-1/2 in framing nail range",
      "Usually among the cheapest name-brand framing nailers",
      "Simple pneumatic design"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Listed at 8.2 to 8.8 lb, heavier than the Metabo HPT",
      "Needs a compressor and hose",
      "Spec details vary between US and UK variants"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "On a tight budget with a compressor already in the garage, the F21PL frames walls and hangs the odd joist hanger for less than any cordless option."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Framing Nailers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-framing-nailer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best framing nailer for a homeowner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homeowners, the best framing nailer is the cordless model on the battery platform they already own, such as the Ryobi PBL345 for Ryobi users or the DeWalt DCN21PL for DeWalt users. If you already own a compressor, a pneumatic nailer like the Metabo HPT NR90AES1 is lighter and much cheaper."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 21 or 30 degree framing nailer better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither is better in general. 21° nailers use plastic-collated full round-head nails, which some codes require for structural work. 30° nailers use paper-collated nails, often clipped-head, and have slightly more compact magazines. Choose based on the nails your plans and local supplier call for."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cordless framing nailers as powerful as pneumatic?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Current cordless framing nailers from major brands are rated to drive full-length framing nails, and the Milwaukee, Metabo HPT and Ryobi picks are listed up to 3-1/2 inches. The main differences are weight, which is higher for cordless, and cost, which is higher up front."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size nails does a framing nailer use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most framing nailers use nails from about 2 to 3-1/4 or 3-1/2 inches, with shank diameters from .113 to .131 or .148 inch. Wall framing commonly uses 3 to 3-1/4 inch nails or 3-1/2 inch 16d commons. Always check your plans or local code for the specified nail."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a compressor for a framing nailer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only for a pneumatic nailer. Cordless battery nailers and fuel-cell nailers like the Paslode do not need a compressor. If you buy pneumatic, make sure your compressor can supply the tool's operating pressure, typically 70 to 120 PSI, at the pace you work."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a framing nailer be used for joist hangers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not usually without a special nosepiece. Joist hangers need short, thick metal connector nails placed precisely through the holes. Some nailers, such as the Bostitch F21PL, ship with a metal-connector nosepiece, otherwise use a positive-placement nailer or drive them by hand."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How heavy is a cordless framing nailer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Listed weights for the cordless picks here range from Paslode's 7.2 lb with battery to nearly 11 lb for the Metabo HPT NR3690DR with a battery, per JLC Online. Pneumatic nailers like the Metabo HPT NR90AES1 are listed at 7 to 7.5 lb without a battery or hose."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best framing nailer",
      "cordless framing nailer",
      "pneumatic framing nailer",
      "21 degree vs 30 degree framing nailer",
      "framing nail gun"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Framing Picks
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Framing Nailers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Cordless, pneumatic and fuel-cell framing nailers compared on the four things that decide the right tool: nail range, collation angle, weight and power source.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#milwaukee-2744-20"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#power-source" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Pneumatic vs Cordless</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#collation-angle" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. 21° vs 30°</a>
          <a href="#nail-range" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Nail Range</a>
          <a href="#weight-and-balance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Weight</a>
          <a href="#firing-modes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Firing Modes</a>
          <a href="#rent-or-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Rent or Buy</a>
          <a href="#safety-maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Safety & Care</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best framing nailer is usually the one that runs on what you already own. If you have a compressor, a pneumatic nailer is lighter and cheaper than any cordless tool. If you have a battery platform, buy that brand's framing nailer. Only then compare nail range, collation angle and weight.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            This list is built from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews, not from a lab test. Every nail range, magazine size, weight and runtime figure below is attributed to the brand, the retailer that listed it, or the publication that measured it, and where two sources disagree we give both numbers rather than pick the more flattering one. Framing nailer weights are the most common disagreement, because some listings quote the bare tool and others include the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things most heavily. First, the nail range, because a framing nailer that cannot drive 3-1/4 inch nails is not much use for wall framing, and some jobs call for 3-1/2 inch nails. Second, the power source, because pneumatic, battery and fuel-cell nailers suit very different owners. Third, the collation angle, which locks you into a nail type for the life of the tool. Fourth, weight, which matters more than almost anything once you are holding the tool above your head on a ladder.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not rank by brand reputation alone, and we did not include no-name nailers whose specs we could not confirm from at least one reputable listing. The picks cover a cordless option for each major battery platform, two pneumatic nailers for compressor owners, and a fuel-cell nailer for people who prize light weight above all.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only build a deck or a shed every few years, read the section on renting versus buying before you spend $300 to $450. If you frame regularly, the battery platform you already own will usually decide the pick for you, and that is a perfectly sensible way to choose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pneumatic, Cordless or Fuel Cell
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pneumatic framing nailers are still the lightest and cheapest tools that drive full-length framing nails. The Metabo HPT NR90AES1 is listed at 7 to 7.5 pounds, while every battery nailer here is listed above 8.5 pounds with its battery and several approach 11 pounds. The trade is the compressor and hose: you need a compressor that can supply enough air for the pace you work, a hose long enough to reach the work, and the patience to keep that hose out from under your feet on a roof.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless battery nailers remove the hose and the compressor noise, which is why they have taken over remodeling and punch-out work. Most use either a flywheel or an air spring to drive the nail. Air-spring designs, like the Metabo HPT NR3690DR, fire with very little delay after the trigger pull; some flywheel designs need a brief moment to spin up. The price is weight and cost, with bare tools running roughly $300 to $450 before batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel-cell nailers, such as the Paslode Cordless XP, burn a small charge of fuel to drive each nail and use a battery only for the spark and fan. Paslode lists its XP at 7.2 pounds with the battery, which makes it the lightest cordless option here. The ongoing cost of fuel cells, the exhaust smell indoors, and the one-strip magazine are the reasons it is not our overall pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple rule works for most buyers. If you own a compressor and frame in one place for hours at a time, buy pneumatic. If you move constantly or work in finished houses, buy cordless on the battery platform you already own. If weight is your main complaint and you are happy to buy fuel cells, look at the Paslode.
          </p>
        </div>

