          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best framing nailer for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, the best framing nailer is the cordless model on the battery platform they already own, such as the Ryobi PBL345 for Ryobi users or the DeWalt DCN21PL for DeWalt users. If you already own a compressor, a pneumatic nailer like the Metabo HPT NR90AES1 is lighter and much cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 21 or 30 degree framing nailer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better in general. 21° nailers use plastic-collated full round-head nails, which some codes require for structural work. 30° nailers use paper-collated nails, often clipped-head, and have slightly more compact magazines. Choose based on the nails your plans and local supplier call for.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless framing nailers as powerful as pneumatic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Current cordless framing nailers from major brands are rated to drive full-length framing nails, and the Milwaukee, Metabo HPT and Ryobi picks are listed up to 3-1/2 inches. The main differences are weight, which is higher for cordless, and cost, which is higher up front.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size nails does a framing nailer use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most framing nailers use nails from about 2 to 3-1/4 or 3-1/2 inches, with shank diameters from .113 to .131 or .148 inch. Wall framing commonly uses 3 to 3-1/4 inch nails or 3-1/2 inch 16d commons. Always check your plans or local code for the specified nail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a compressor for a framing nailer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for a pneumatic nailer. Cordless battery nailers and fuel-cell nailers like the Paslode do not need a compressor. If you buy pneumatic, make sure your compressor can supply the tool's operating pressure, typically 70 to 120 PSI, at the pace you work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a framing nailer be used for joist hangers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually without a special nosepiece. Joist hangers need short, thick metal connector nails placed precisely through the holes. Some nailers, such as the Bostitch F21PL, ship with a metal-connector nosepiece, otherwise use a positive-placement nailer or drive them by hand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy is a cordless framing nailer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Listed weights for the cordless picks here range from Paslode's 7.2 lb with battery to nearly 11 lb for the Metabo HPT NR3690DR with a battery, per JLC Online. Pneumatic nailers like the Metabo HPT NR90AES1 are listed at 7 to 7.5 lb without a battery or hose.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

